[FINAL-TEXT] **Root cause found!** Router2's iptables/nftables FORWARD chain has explicit DROP rules targeting source subnets 192.168.1.0/24 (IT) and 192.168.2.0/24 (CS):

```
ip saddr 192.168.1.0/24 counter packets 20 bytes 1536 drop
ip saddr 192.168.2.0/24 counter packets 3 bytes 252 drop
```

The hit counters already show packets being dropped — those are exactly the pings I ran during this investigation. This perfectly explains the asymmetric symptom: IT/CS traffic (source 192.168.1.x or 192.168.2.x) gets forwarded from the WAN side (eth2) toward the Server/Internet LANs (eth0/eth1) and is dropped at router2's FORWARD chain. Office/Principle (192.168.3.x, 192.168.4.x) are not in the DROP rules and pass freely. IT/CS hosts can still reach router2's own interfaces (ICMP TTL-exceeded, pings to 1.0.0.1/20.20.0.2) because those go through router2's INPUT chain, not FORWARD. The ticket's hypothesis (ripd died) is incorrect — ripd is running on all three routers.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The fault is an nftables packet-filter rule injected into router2's FORWARD chain, not a dead RIP daemon as the ticket claimed. Router2 has two explicit DROP rules in `table ip filter / chain FORWARD` targeting source subnets 192.168.1.0/24 (IT LAN) and 192.168.2.0/24 (CS LAN). Any packet originating from those subnets that router2 would normally forward onward — from the WAN interface (eth2, 20.20.0.2) toward the Server Room LAN (eth0, 1.0.0.0/8) or Internet Lab LAN (eth1, 128.168.0.0/16) — is silently dropped. Hit counters (20 packets/1536 bytes for IT; 3 packets/252 bytes for CS) confirm live drops.

The asymmetry arises because Office (192.168.3.x) and Principle (192.168.4.x) source addresses are absent from the DROP rules and transit router2 freely. IT/CS hosts can still reach router2's own IP addresses (ICMP replies traverse router2's INPUT chain, not FORWARD), which explains why traceroute shows hop 3 = 20.20.0.2 responding while hop 4 (pc1) is silent — the forwarded probe is dropped at router2's nftables FORWARD hook before reaching pc1.

RIP is actually healthy: all three routers run ripd (started by startup script; systemctl shows "inactive" but the daemons are live), and routing tables are fully populated on all routers. The root cause is a firewall misconfiguration on router2 — two nftables FORWARD DROP rules block all packets sourced from the IT and CS subnets.

Corrective fix: On router2, delete the two DROP rules from `table ip filter chain FORWARD` via `nft flush chain ip filter FORWARD` (or delete the individual rules by handle), then verify with `nft list ruleset`. No RIP restart is needed.
=== END ===